window.docContents[230].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___19-Maclaurin-Series-and-Laurent-Series/#chapter-4-section-19":{"contents":"Source: NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §4.19.\n\nThe trigonometric functions have entire Maclaurin series for sine and cosine,\nwhile tangent and secant have their nearest poles at ±π/2.  Cosecant and\ncotangent are represented by Laurent series with their principal parts at the\norigin.  The final three displays are power series for the principal complex\nlogarithm.  In every entry, HasSum records both the coefficient sequence and\nits convergence to the stated function value.  The Bernoulli coefficients use\nMathlib's canonical bernoulli sequence (with B₀=1 and B₁=-1/2).  The\nEuler coefficients use the transparent DLMF recurrence: E₀=1, odd Euler\nnumbers vanish, and each positive even Eₙ is the negative binomial sum of\nthe preceding even values.\n\n\n\n\n\\sin z=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^n z^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.\nThis series converges for every complex z, so sine is entire.\n\n\n\\cos z=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^n z^{2n}}{(2n)!}.\nThe cosine series is also entire and converges for every complex z.\n\n\n\\tan z=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}2^{2n}(2^{2n}-1)B_{2n}}{(2n)!}z^{2n-1},\n\\qquad |z|<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\nThe tangent series is a power series about zero with radius π/2; its\ncoefficient is written using the Bernoulli numbers B₂ₙ.\n\n\n\\csc z=\\frac1z+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}2(2^{2n-1}-1)B_{2n}}{(2n)!}z^{2n-1},\n\\qquad 0<|z|<\\pi.\nThis is a Laurent series on the punctured disk 0 < |z| < π; the explicit\n1/z is its principal part at the origin.\n\n\n\\sec z=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^nE_{2n}}{(2n)!}z^{2n},\n\\qquad |z|<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\nThe Euler numbers E₂ₙ give the even-power secant series, convergent in the\ndisk bounded by the nearest poles.\n\n\n\\cot z=\\frac1z-\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}2^{2n}B_{2n}}{(2n)!}z^{2n-1},\n\\qquad 0<|z|<\\pi.\nAs with cosecant, the cotangent expansion is Laurent rather than Maclaurin:\nthe 1/z term is separated from the convergent series on the punctured disk.\n\n\n\\ln\\left(\\frac{\\sin z}{z}\\right)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\frac{(-1)^n2^{2n-1}B_{2n}}{n(2n)!}z^{2n},\n\\qquad |z|<\\pi.\nHere ln denotes the principal complex logarithm.  The disk |z|<π is the\nconvergence domain of the even-power expansion.  The Lean sinRatio name\nextends sin z/z at the removable center by setting sinRatio 0 = 1, so the\nleft side is explicitly log 1 = 0 at z=0.\n\n\n\\ln(\\cos z)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\frac{(-1)^n2^{2n-1}(2^{2n}-1)B_{2n}}{n(2n)!}z^{2n},\n\\qquad |z|<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\nThe principal-logarithm series for cosine stops at the nearest zero of cosine,\ngiving the radius π/2.\n\n\n\\ln\\left(\\frac{\\tan z}{z}\\right)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}2^{2n}(2^{2n-1}-1)B_{2n}}{n(2n)!}z^{2n},\n\\qquad |z|<\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\nThe final expansion is again for the principal logarithm, now of the\neven analytic quotient tan z / z; its disk ends at |z|=π/2.  The Lean\ntanRatio definition makes the removable center explicit with tanRatio 0 = 1,\nso the logarithm at zero is again 0.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions","header":"4.19. §4.19 Maclaurin Series and Laurent Series","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___19-Maclaurin-Series-and-Laurent-Series/#chapter-4-section-19"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___29-Graphics/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___29-Graphics--___4___29-Graphics--___4___29_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Real-arguments":{"contents":"The DLMF notes that these graphs were produced at NIST. Figure 4.29.1 plots\nthe real functions sinh x and cosh x; the first disclosure records the\nstrict positivity of cosh and the ordering -cosh x < sinh x < cosh x.\nFigure 4.29.2 plots the principal values of arcsinh x and arccosh x;\nfor real arguments, cosh has range [1,∞), so the inverse cosine is real\nthere and complex below 1. Figure 4.29.3 plots tanh x and coth x:\ntanh stays strictly between -1 and 1, while coth is its reciprocal away\nfrom the pole at zero. Figure 4.29.4 displays the corresponding principal\ninverse values and the real coth range outside the unit interval.  The\ncomplex coth preimage statement below is restricted away from zeros of sinh,\nso Lean's totalized value at a pole is not treated as an inverse value.\n\nFigures 4.29.5 and 4.29.6 show csch/sech and their principal inverses.\nThe real graph has sinh x = 0 exactly at the csch pole, whereas cosh x\nnever vanishes; consequently sech x takes values in (0,1]. The six\nfigure disclosures below are honest real-argument statements about the\nplotted branches, and do not pretend that a picture is a numbered identity.\n\nThe plotted real branches satisfy the strict ordering and positivity shown in Figure 4.29.1.\n\nThe real part of the principal arccosh graph is supported by the real range of cosh, while the plotted inverse is complex below 1.\n\nFigure 4.29.3 records the bounded tanh branch and the reciprocal coth branch away from its pole.\n\nThe real coth range is strictly outside the closed unit interval: the boundary values ±1 are limiting values and are not attained at finite nonzero real arguments. Its inverse graph is complex on the interior interval; the formal complex preimage clause below requires sinh y ≠ 0 so that a totalized pole value is not counted. The arctanh graph is complex outside the closed unit interval.\n\nThe csch pole and the everywhere-finite sech denominator are stated explicitly for Figure 4.29.5.\n\nFigure 4.29.6 uses the principal inverse branches of csch and sech; the real sech range is (0,1], so the principal arcsech branch is complex for targets x < 0 or x > 1.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.29 Graphics\u0009§4.29 Graphics","header":"§4.29(i) Real arguments","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___29-Graphics/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___29-Graphics--___4___29-Graphics--___4___29_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Real-arguments"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___3-Graphics/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___3-Graphics--___4___3-Graphics--___4___3_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Real-Arguments":{"contents":"Source: DLMF §4.3(i).\n\n\\ln x\\quad\\text{and}\\quad {\\mathrm e}^{x}. The curves are mirror images in the line y=x: their parallel tangents at\n(1,0) and (0,1) make the inverse relationship visible.  On the real line,\nlog and exp are inverse everywhere in the direction log (exp x) = x, and\nthe reverse direction holds on the positive half-line.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.3 Graphics\u0009§4.3 Graphics","header":"§4.3(i) Real Arguments","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___3-Graphics/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___3-Graphics--___4___3-Graphics--___4___3_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Real-Arguments"}});